1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:03,374
What if you could fix a bug just by writing a comment on GitHub?

2
00:00:03,824 --> 00:00:10,815
With the Claude Code GitHub Action, you type @claude in an issue, and Claude reads your code, fixes it, and pushes the change.

3
00:00:11,265 --> 00:00:19,989
This video is a lab. I'll explain how it works, then we'll set it up together on a real repository. Every file is linked in the description, so you can follow along.

4
00:00:21,539 --> 00:00:22,993
So, what is the Claude Code Action?

5
00:00:23,443 --> 00:00:29,523
It's a GitHub Action, an automated job that GitHub runs for you, that starts Claude Code inside your repository.

6
00:00:29,973 --> 00:00:37,549
You mention @claude in an issue or a pull request. Claude reads the code, makes changes, commits them, and replies in a comment.

7
00:00:37,999 --> 00:00:41,386
It all runs on GitHub's servers. Your laptop can be closed.

8
00:00:41,836 --> 00:00:47,234
And the setup is just three pieces: a workflow file, the Claude GitHub App, and one secret.

9
00:00:48,684 --> 00:00:52,116
The action has two modes, and it picks one from your workflow file.

10
00:00:52,566 --> 00:00:59,168
Interactive mode: if you don't give it a prompt, Claude waits for someone to write @claude in a comment, then answers that request.

11
00:00:59,618 --> 00:01:06,331
Automation mode: if you give it a prompt, it runs on its own, for example on every new pull request, or every morning.

12
00:01:06,781 --> 00:01:14,908
In both modes, only people with write access can trigger it, and bots are blocked. And under the hood, it's the same Claude Code you use in your terminal.

13
00:01:16,358 --> 00:01:20,155
What can you ask it to do? Answer questions about your code, right in the issue.

14
00:01:20,605 --> 00:01:25,320
Fix bugs, and build features from an issue description, then push the changes to a branch.

15
00:01:25,770 --> 00:01:28,058
Review pull requests and comment on the code.

16
00:01:28,508 --> 00:01:33,334
Run on a schedule, like a daily summary of commits. And run your own skills or plugins.

17
00:01:34,784 --> 00:01:35,615
When should you use it?

18
00:01:36,065 --> 00:01:45,236
Use the action for small, clearly described tasks, reviews on every pull request, and recurring jobs. Especially when your team already lives in GitHub.

19
00:01:45,686 --> 00:01:51,096
Stay in your terminal for big, open-ended changes, or when you want to steer every step yourself.

20
00:01:52,546 --> 00:01:57,247
Here's our lab: a small shop project with a shipping bug, reported in a GitHub issue.

21
00:01:57,697 --> 00:02:04,777
Normally, you read the issue, pull the code, find the bug, fix it, push, and open a pull request. By hand.

22
00:02:05,227 --> 00:02:10,815
With the action, you write one comment. Claude finds the cause and pushes the fix. You just review it.

23
00:02:12,365 --> 00:02:16,353
Here's the starter project. You can copy it from the GitHub link in the description.

24
00:02:16,803 --> 00:02:22,584
shipping.py has one rule: orders of fifty dollars or more ship free. But look at this line.

25
00:02:23,034 --> 00:02:28,063
Let's run the tests. One fails: an order of exactly fifty is still charged shipping.

26
00:02:28,513 --> 00:02:35,412
The project also has a Tests workflow, so GitHub runs these tests on every push. That's how we'll prove Claude's fix works.

27
00:02:35,862 --> 00:02:42,255
And a CLAUDE.md file with project rules. The action reads it on every run, just like Claude Code does locally.

28
00:02:43,105 --> 00:02:45,207
Step one: put the project on GitHub.

29
00:02:45,657 --> 00:02:50,615
I commit the files, then create a new repository with the GitHub command line, and push.

30
00:02:51,065 --> 00:02:55,287
You need to be an admin of the repo for the next steps. On your own repo, you are.

31
00:02:56,637 --> 00:03:02,956
Step two: install the Claude GitHub App. It lets Claude comment, push branches, and work with pull requests.

32
00:03:03,406 --> 00:03:06,300
I click install, and choose only the lab repository.

33
00:03:06,750 --> 00:03:15,696
Claude mainly needs read and write access to contents, issues and pull requests. The app asks for a few more permissions, used by other Claude features.

34
00:03:16,146 --> 00:03:26,170
Tip: there's also a shortcut. Run slash install-github-app inside Claude Code, and it does steps two to four for you. Here, we do it by hand, so you see every piece.

35
00:03:27,520 --> 00:03:31,159
Step three: add a secret, so the action can log in to Claude.

36
00:03:31,609 --> 00:03:37,861
With a Pro or Max subscription, run claude setup-token. It creates a long-lived token for your subscription.

37
00:03:38,311 --> 00:03:43,937
Then I save it as a repository secret named CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN, and paste the token when asked.

38
00:03:44,387 --> 00:03:50,151
Secrets are encrypted by GitHub. The workflow can use them, but nobody can read them back. Not even you.

39
00:03:50,601 --> 00:03:55,986
Prefer an API key from the Claude Console? Name the secret ANTHROPIC_API_KEY instead.

40
00:03:56,836 --> 00:04:00,509
Step four: add the workflow file. This is the heart of the setup.

41
00:04:00,959 --> 00:04:05,615
Workflows live in dot github slash workflows. I call this one claude.yml.

42
00:04:06,065 --> 00:04:11,961
The on section says when it runs: every new comment, on an issue, or on a pull request review.

43
00:04:12,411 --> 00:04:17,537
This if line skips every comment that doesn't mention @claude, so you don't waste runner minutes.

44
00:04:17,987 --> 00:04:29,901
Permissions: write access to contents, pull requests and issues, so Claude can push and comment. Id-token is needed to log in as the Claude app. And actions read lets Claude see your CI results.

45
00:04:30,351 --> 00:04:33,203
The checkout step gives Claude a copy of your code to work on.

46
00:04:34,001 --> 00:04:38,709
And here's the action itself: anthropics slash claude-code-action, version one.

47
00:04:40,684 --> 00:04:44,669
It reads the token from our secret. Never paste the token itself in this file.

48
00:04:45,119 --> 00:04:52,695
Claude args passes normal Claude Code options. Max turns ten caps how much work one run can do, which keeps costs under control.

49
00:04:53,145 --> 00:04:57,530
There's no prompt line. So this is interactive mode: it waits for @claude.

50
00:04:58,280 --> 00:05:02,407
I commit the workflow and push it. Workflows only run once they're on GitHub.

51
00:05:03,757 --> 00:05:09,630
Step five: test it. On GitHub, I open an issue describing the bug, just like a customer report.

52
00:05:10,080 --> 00:05:13,003
Then I add a comment: @claude, please fix this bug.

53
00:05:13,453 --> 00:05:17,993
Within seconds, Claude replies with a progress comment, and a link to the running job.

54
00:05:18,443 --> 00:05:27,915
In the Actions tab, you can watch the Claude Code workflow run. It checks out the code, starts Claude, and Claude reads CLAUDE.md, the issue, and shipping.py.

55
00:05:28,365 --> 00:05:37,697
About a minute later, the comment updates. Claude explains the cause in one sentence, exactly as our CLAUDE.md asked, and pushes the fix to a new branch.

56
00:05:38,147 --> 00:05:42,048
Its reply links to the branch, so I create the pull request with one click.

57
00:05:42,498 --> 00:05:50,360
And here's the proof. The pull request changes one character, greater than becomes greater or equal, and the Tests workflow is now green.

58
00:05:50,810 --> 00:05:55,712
Claude fixed the code, not the test, as our rules said. I review it, and merge.

59
00:05:56,462 --> 00:06:05,679
You can also talk to it inside the pull request. For example, @claude, review this change, or @claude, add a test for an order of forty-nine ninety-nine.

60
00:06:07,229 --> 00:06:11,012
Back on my computer, I pull the merged fix, and run the tests once more.

61
00:06:11,462 --> 00:06:16,239
All green. Bug reported, fixed and verified, without me opening an editor.

62
00:06:17,789 --> 00:06:19,324
Before you go, five tips.

63
00:06:19,774 --> 00:06:25,543
One: write a CLAUDE.md. Claude reads it on every run, so keep it short and specific.

64
00:06:25,993 --> 00:06:33,732
Two: cap every run, with max turns, and a timeout on the job. Each run uses GitHub Actions minutes, and Claude usage.

65
00:06:34,182 --> 00:06:38,355
Three: keep tokens and keys in secrets. Never in the workflow file.

66
00:06:38,805 --> 00:06:48,716
Four: add a prompt input, and the same action runs on its own, on every pull request, or on a schedule. The examples folder of the action's repo has ready-made workflows.

67
00:06:49,166 --> 00:06:53,364
And five: Claude pushes to branches. You still review and merge.

68
00:06:54,914 --> 00:06:55,539
Let's recap.

69
00:06:55,989 --> 00:06:59,491
The Claude Code Action runs Claude Code inside your GitHub workflows.

70
00:06:59,941 --> 00:07:04,321
You set it up with the GitHub App, one secret, and a claude.yml workflow file.

71
00:07:04,771 --> 00:07:08,116
Then you comment @claude, review what it pushes, and merge.

72
00:07:09,566 --> 00:07:16,265
Now it's your turn. The starter project and a step-by-step lab guide are linked in the description. It takes about fifteen minutes.

73
00:07:16,715 --> 00:07:23,162
If this helped, like the video, and subscribe to Zero Cost AI for more free AI tutorials. See you in the next one!

